  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Coprinellus flocculosus, commonly known as the flocculose inkcap, is a small, delicate mushroom characterized by its distinctive cottony or floccose (woolly) coating on the cap. This ephemeral fungus belongs to the Psathyrellaceae family and is known for its tendency to deliquesce, or dissolve into an inky liquid as it matures.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Floccose Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap is covered with white to grayish cottony or woolly fibrils that give it a distinctive fuzzy appearance, easily distinguishing it from smooth-capped relatives.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Deliquescing Gills",
      "description": "Gills darken from white to black and liquefy into an inky liquid as the mushroom matures, a characteristic shared with other inkcap species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Fragile Stature",
      "description": "The mushroom is relatively small (1-2 inches tall) with a thin, brittle stem that is white to grayish and breaks easily.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Flocculose inkcaps are found fruiting on decaying wood, wood chips, and disturbed soil in woodlands and gardens throughout New England, particularly during moist spring and fall conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other inkcaps, Coprinellus flocculosus has a remarkably short fruiting lifespan, often maturing and liquefying within hours, which is why they are rarely encountered unless searched for at dawn when they first emerge.",
